From the formula, we can see that the gravitational force is directly proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. For Earth and asteroid A, since asteroid A has less mass than Earth, and since mass is a positive quantity in this context, the only variable we care about for asteroids of less mass than Earth is the distance from Earth.

In Rob's case, asteroid A is farther from Earth than asteroid B, C, or D, regardless of their respective masses. Because the gravitational force decreases with distance (\( r^2 \) in the denominator of the formula), even if asteroid B is closer to Earth, Earth will still exert a stronger gravitational pull than asteroid A or any other asteroid mentioned.

So, for the given scenario, Earth will have a stronger gravitational pull than asteroid A, or any of the other asteroids, given that they all have less mass than Earth. The gravitational pull of the Earth vastly exceeds that of any asteroid, both because of its much larger mass and its proximity to any object on its surface or in near-Earth space.
