The origin of the fat Buddha and laughing Buddha can be traced back to the Song Dynasty in China (960-1279 CE) when a Chinese monk named Budai was mistakenly identified as the Buddha by Japanese people. Budai, known for his plump figure and joyful nature, was a jovial monk who enjoyed eating and drinking, which contrasted sharply with the austere image of the historical Buddha. This misidentification, which arose from cultural exchanges and interpretations, led to the creation of the fat Buddha and laughing Buddha figures in Chinese and Japanese cultures, respectively, becoming beloved symbols of joy and abundance.
