second_to_last = extract_second_to_last_element(my_list)
print(second_to_last)  # Output: 4
```

In this solution, we first calculate the length of the given list. Then, if the length is at least 2, we use negative indexing (`-2`) to access the second-to-last element. Otherwise, if the list has fewer than 2 elements, we handle that case appropriately (e.g., returning `None` or any other desired value).

Note that negative indexing starts from the last element (-1) and counts backward, so -2 refers to the second-to-last element, -3 refers to the third-to-last element, and so on.
