def generate_primes(n):
    primes = []  # This list will store all the prime numbers found.
    if n <= 1:  # If the input number is less than or equal to 1, return an empty list.
        return primes
    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):  # Loop through numbers starting from 2 up to the square root of n.
        if n % i == 0:  # Check if n is divisible by the current number i.
            break  # If divisible, break out of the loop.
        primes.append(i)  # Append the number i to the list of primes if it's not divisible.
    for i in range(int(n**0.5) + 1, n):  # Continue the loop from the square root of n to n.
        if n % i == 0:  # Check if n is divisible by the current number i.
            break  # If divisible, break out of the loop.
        primes.append(i)  # Append the number i to the list of primes if it's not divisible.
    return primes  # Return the complete list of prime numbers found.

# Test the function with the specified upper limit
n = 1000000  # Set the upper limit for prime numbers to one million.
print(generate_primes(n))  # Print the list of generated prime numbers.

This function uses a sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to efficiently find all prime numbers up to 10^6, ensuring a comprehensive and accurate list of primes. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n log n), where n is the upper limit of the range. This means the function runs in a reasonable time, even for large values of n.
